{
    "id": 79,
    "title": "W0 Indexer, site side (corrected): every URL found logged, the machine classifies sites, shipped switched off",
    "why": "Replaces #78, whose migration MySQL refused (stored is a reserved word in MySQL 8; crawl_batches.stored is now stored_n). Otherwise the same release. Jason, 2026-11-17 (decisions/0164 §1.4's note: \"It should log all urls it finds because it will be identified at a later step\"; \"Put it first in the queue\"). 1) The frontier: every link on every page read is logged (normalized, de-duplicated, with its page and link text), same site or not; the old caps (60 links a page, 40 other sites a read) are gone on the site; a safety net flags a page with an absurd number of links, nothing dropped. 2) No 'Waiting for you' gate: the machine classifies every site by its address and what its home page says (tier 1-5, kind, status); chambers, tourism boards and organizations are Read regularly, their facts kept only as licence and terms allow (Jason's OK, 2026-11-17); commercial and social cite only; Facebook and Instagram never read; a tribal nation's site stays a person's call (R7); nothing already decided changes; today's waiting sites are classified by the hourly upkeep on the first pass. 3) The verdict loop: promoted, normal, demoted, junk on each URL and site, junk kept as its hash so it's never logged or read twice, frequent junk patterns become skip rules, every verdict in crawl_audit_log with Undo; a person's verdict outranks the machine's. 4) Reading priority: places we have first, then tier and verdict, links in, depth from a trusted source. 5) Size guard: a URL never read in 180 days keeps only its hash; size and growth recorded. 6) /api/crawl/index.php for the always-on worker (Release 2): work out, batches in, each with a receipt (stored count and checksum), keyed so a resend changes nothing; three mismatches in a row open a Pulse case. 7) W0 is its own workflow (switched off), first in Start the system; Pause everything stops it (the master); its budget line under Railway in the Service register; its settings in Thresholds. The key is sha1 (the page log's), not SHA-256 (Jason's OK). Tested on MariaDB: tools/w0-tests/frontier.php, 55 of 55.",
